WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
React Native's new architecture entirely eliminates the asynchronous communication bridge,

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.400
replacing it with a fully synchronous, bridgeless environment.

3
00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:13.880
That shift makes the monolithic legacy chrome remote debugger obsolete.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.160
It can no longer intercept the traffic.

5
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.000
Debugging is no longer a centralized process.

6
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.960
You are now working across a multi-tier architecture,

7
00:00:21.960 --> 00:00:25.600
and diagnosing issues requires a domain-specific approach.

8
00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:28.760
This diagram represents the new execution stack.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.000
At the top, we have the JavaScript and React layer.

10
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.280
In the middle, sits the Hermes engine and the JSI layer.

11
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:39.080
At the base, we have native Kotlin and the Android operating system.

12
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.880
No single tool can see all three of these layers at the same time.

13
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:47.200
To cover the entire stack, you need an exact set of diagnostic tools.

14
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.640
React Native Dev Tools, the Chrome Dev Tools Protocol,

15
00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.800
Flipper, Android Studio, CIS Trace, and Logbox.

16
00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:57.880
You must construct a new debugging matrix,

17
00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:01.720
mapping specific tools to exact architectural layers

18
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:05.480
in order to successfully isolate performance bottlenecks.

19
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.160
We start our analysis at the top of the stack, JavaScript and React.

20
00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.400
For modern versions of React Rative,

21
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.600
the first party default debugger is React Native Dev Tools.

22
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.600
This interface handles your standard console logging

23
00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.560
and allows you to inspect the React component tree

24
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.560
while monitoring the live state of your props.

25
00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.840
TypeScript source maps route directly into this tool,

26
00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:31.240
meaning you can set breakpoints in your source files

27
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.800
and step through a synchronous code with accurate file and line tracking.

28
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.720
When you need to inspect the network layer or the UI view hierarchy,

29
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.240
you switch to Flickr.

30
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.160
Flipper's layout inspector connects your React components

31
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.960
directly to the underlying yoga layout engine,

32
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.360
revealing exact margin, padding, and size data.

33
00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.560
React Native Dev Tools and Flipper

34
00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.400
handle the developer experience at this top layer exclusively.

35
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.680
They are blind to execution issues happening deeper in the engine.

36
00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:04.480
Moving one layer down the stack, we reach the Hermes engine.

37
00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:07.680
Advanced profiling at this level requires connecting directly

38
00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.880
to Hermes via the Chrome Dev Tools Protocol or CDP.

39
00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.040
This timeline illustrates finding memory leaks using heap snapshots.

40
00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:19.680
Take a baseline snapshot at state A, navigate away,

41
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.280
and take a second snapshot at state B.

42
00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.240
Comparing the two reveals persistent objects.

43
00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.240
These are allocations failing collection

44
00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.240
by the Hermes garbage collector.

45
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.640
CDP also provides a CPU profiler to identify

46
00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:36.320
synchronous JavaScript execution blocks that delay the thread.

47
00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.960
In this context, any execution block exceeding

48
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.200
50 milliseconds on the main thread is categorized as a long task.

49
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.880
By isolating the JavaScript engine with CDP,

50
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:49.040
you prevent the misdiagnosis of blaming the native device hardware

51
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.240
for computation delays happening strictly in your code.

52
00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:54.680
We drop to the bottom layer of the architecture,

53
00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:57.200
native Kotlin and the operating system.

54
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:59.960
The Hermes tools cannot penetrate this far down,

55
00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.600
making Android Studio mandatory.

56
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:06.240
This is an Android Studio CPU profiler flame chart

57
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:10.000
used for diagnosing the performance of custom turbo modules.

58
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.040
By tracking custom trace sections placed in your code,

59
00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.200
this chart reveals the exact microsecond execution times

60
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.160
for your Kotlin methods.

61
00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.320
For diagnosing memory issues specifically

62
00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.480
within fabric components, you switch

63
00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:25.160
to Android Studio's memory profiler.

64
00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:26.760
The workflow is direct.

65
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.560
You navigate to the screen holding your fabric component

66
00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:32.200
then manually force garbage collection.

67
00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.800
After generating a heap dump,

68
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.000
you filter the results to search for your specific UI class,

69
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.480
such as an abstract compose view.

70
00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.800
If that class lingers in memory after the view

71
00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.560
has been detached from the screen,

72
00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:45.720
the native component is leaking.

73
00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:48.480
This happens when callbacks or listeners are not properly

74
00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:50.440
cleared out in the Kotlin code.

75
00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:54.000
The native layer is no longer a hidden execution environment.

76
00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.000
Mastering Android Studio is now a core requirement

77
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.040
for developers working with custom modules

78
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:00.520
on the new architecture.

79
00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:03.240
Finally, we zoom out to view the entire system

80
00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:04.720
interacting over time.

81
00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:07.840
CIS Trace is the primary tool for diagnosing dropped frames

82
00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.120
and UI jank.

83
00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.760
This timeline reveals macro execution.

84
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.360
The choreographer track maintains a 16 millisecond window.

85
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:18.440
A gap here definitively identifies a dropped frame.

86
00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:20.840
Tracing vertically down, a blocked render thread

87
00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:23.560
means computationally heavy fabric layouts.

88
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:25.720
Further down, massive JS thread blocks

89
00:04:25.720 --> 00:04:28.640
reveal synchronous execution delaying the render.

90
00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:30.760
CIS Trace provides the definitive evidence

91
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:33.480
of how these layers interact, exposing exactly

92
00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.880
where the bottleneck occurs during a frame drop.

93
00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:38.520
As you migrate older applications to this architecture,

94
00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.640
you will operate in a transition state.

95
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.240
Legacy native modules attempting

96
00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:45.200
to use the old bridge APIs in a bridgeless environment

97
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.040
will trigger warnings or errors.

98
00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.040
You monitor the log box structured error overlay,

99
00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.840
specifically for these compatibility warnings.

100
00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:56.520
The immediate solution is to update the library dependencies

101
00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:58.640
or utilize the built-in interop layer

102
00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.680
to run older modules in compatibility mode.

103
00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:04.560
Returning to our three-tier architectural stack,

104
00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.320
we now have a complete map of our diagnostic tools

105
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:09.400
locked into their correct layers.

106
00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:11.160
The routing rules are clear.

107
00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:13.640
UI frame drops require CIS Trace.

108
00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.480
Kotlin memory issues require Android Studio.

109
00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:19.600
And JavaScript engine lockups require CDP.

110
00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:23.080
The new architecture demands native level engineering rigor.

111
00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.280
You must master domain-specific tools

112
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.240
to diagnose and optimize the full stack.

